B — retention loop
(working-memory detail ↔ short-term fidelity ↔ long-term durability)
and we're asking whether abstraction belongs to a third loop C, rather than simply being the high end of A or B.
Your environmental contrast is then peculiar:
long protected immaturity + social exposure ← vertebrate route
versus
short lifespan + little/no parental knowledge transfer ← coleoid route
Those look almost opposite. And that makes your proposed trade-off interesting.
If an organism has a very short lifetime, extremely elaborate durable memory has a reduced potential payoff simply because there is less future in which stored information can be exploited. Selection might instead favor extraordinarily fast acquisition, immediate discrimination and online problem-solving. Conversely, a long-lived organism with years of protected development can profit enormously from durable storage because information acquired early can continue producing returns for decades.
But now consider abstraction.
Abstraction can benefit both strategies, for different reasons.
For the long-lived social learner, abstraction lets experiences become generalized knowledge:
many particular encounters → common relation → reusable concept.
For the short-lived organism, abstraction could instead function as computational compression:
novel situation → extract structural similarity → solve without requiring an enormous library of previously stored particulars.
That would make abstraction unusually valuable at both extremes.
And I think that's where your prediction about memory follows.
If C contains abstraction, while A and B are separate reinforcing loops, then we shouldn't expect abstraction and memory to covary automatically. In fact, the cephalopod comparison might reveal their independence particularly cleanly:
high abstraction + comparatively shallow durable memory could be viable if abstraction continuously regenerates useful solutions;
whereas another lineage could develop:
high durable memory + comparatively low abstraction, producing enormous learned repertoires without correspondingly powerful relational generalization.
That second category is the critical control. If we can find animals with excellent memory but relatively weak abstraction, then memory cannot simply be treated as an ingredient whose quantitative improvement eventually becomes intelligence. It belongs to a different developmental/evolutionary dimension.
And such dissociations aren't crazy. Some food-caching birds, for example, possess extraordinary spatial memory. That does not automatically imply that every such species has corvid-level domain-general reasoning. Likewise, animals can show spectacular recognition or associative learning within ecologically important domains without displaying equivalently spectacular transfer to novel relational problems.
So your architecture makes a fairly strong comparative prediction:
Across species, memory sophistication should cluster independently of abstraction sophistication more strongly than conventional "general intelligence" accounts would lead us to expect.
And birds become exceptionally useful now.
Because within birds we can potentially hold enormous amounts of evolutionary architecture relatively constant while finding different emphases:
food-caching specialists → unusually strong retention systems;
highly social/vocal-learning species → substantial learned social information;
corvids/parrots → unusually impressive transfer, relational reasoning and behavioral flexibility;
precocial species → much less prolonged helpless social development.
Instead of comparing human vs octopus—where virtually everything differs—we can use birds to start pulling the variables apart.
But there's an even more interesting consequence of what you just proposed.
If abstraction is C, we need to ask what the other members of C are.
You've been constructing each loop not as a named faculty but as a sequence of mutually reinforcing capacities. So "abstraction" shouldn't constitute the third loop by itself. There should be capacities on either side of it whose improvement increases abstraction's utility and whose utility abstraction reciprocally increases.
And the environmental variable you've identified gives us a clue.
Both socially embedded prolonged development and extremely compressed lifespan create, in opposite ways, a premium on extracting generalizable structure rather than accumulating an unlimited collection of particulars.
One because there is an enormous amount of information to integrate.
The other because there is very little time in which to acquire it.
So I would resist filling C in yet.
Because if the architecture is genuinely there, we shouldn't decide what belongs beside abstraction because it makes a nice theory. We should look for capacities that independently covary with abstraction across cephalopods, cognitively exceptional birds, cetaceans, primates—and crucially, animals that are excellent at A or B but apparently poor at abstraction.
If that yields two additional variables, we'd have something much more interesting than an explanation constructed around intelligent animals: we'd have a candidate third reflexive subsystem whose existence was predicted by the structure before its components were identified.
And that's testable.
Yes. And there's an important distinction to keep in mind. These capacities may not all reinforce one another within a single recursively closed loop (individual animal). The total sequence of a single reflexive loop/capacity sequence might begin internally and be closed within the feedback reinforcement structure only through interactions with specific environmental variables, especially other animals of the same species. Or perhaps even aspects of the animal that we normally consider separately from its individual lifespan; aspects such as....genetics?
Yes. That changes what the boundary of the system is. And I think genetics is exactly the dangerous-to-overlook possibility here.
We've implicitly been drawing the recursion around one organism:
organism at t₁ → experience → changed organism at t₂ → experience → changed organism at t₃
But there's no biological requirement that an evolutionary feedback loop close within one nervous system—or even within one lifetime.
The relevant closure could instead be:
individual → environment/conspecifics → reproductive consequences → population genetics → development of next individual → individual
In that case, something that looks nonrecursive when examining one animal becomes perfectly recursive when the unit of analysis is the lineage interacting with its environment across generations.
And cephalopods make this distinction particularly consequential.
Suppose, just as a deliberately extreme model, an octopus possesses fantastic online abstraction but relatively specialized memory. It spends its short life repeatedly solving problems rather than accumulating an enormous autobiographical model. Successful variants reproduce. What gets transmitted isn't what that octopus learned; it's the developmental architecture that made learning/reconstruction possible.
So:
genome → nervous-system architecture → behavior → differential survival/reproduction → changed allele distribution → nervous-system architecture...
That's an ordinary evolutionary feedback process in one sense. But placed inside the architecture we're constructing, genetic inheritance could function as one of the closure mechanisms for a cognitive capacity loop.
And humans might close an analogous loop somewhere completely different.
A human infant's internally incomplete capacities encounter other humans. Those humans supply language, imitation targets, correction, emotional responses, accumulated concepts, tools, conventions, etc. The child's cognition changes; its changed cognition alters subsequent interactions; those interactions feed back again.
So some portion of a human cognitive loop might literally run:
brain → another brain → brain.
Neither individual contains the entire causal circuit.
That means "sociality causes intelligence" would again be too crude. Conspecifics can constitute components of the developmental feedback architecture through which certain cognitive capacities become established.
And then culture creates an even stranger possibility:
brain → external representation → later brain.
Writing makes part of the cognitive-developmental feedback circuit survive the death of the nervous system that produced it.
Which brings genetics and culture into an interesting symmetry
Not equivalence—very different mechanisms—but structurally comparable transmission channels.
Genetic inheritance preserves information about architectures that successfully generated behavior.
Social transmission preserves some behavior/information acquired during individual lives.
Symbolic culture preserves increasingly abstract products of cognition with very high fidelity.
So a reflexive capacity sequence could potentially close over several characteristic timescales:
milliseconds–seconds: neural feedback
minutes–years: learning/memory
years–lifetimes: social developmental feedback
generations: cultural transmission
many generations: genetic evolution
And those loops can be nested rather than parallel.
That's important for your proposed multiplicative architecture. What initially appeared to be three loops inside an intelligent animal might actually be three loops operating at completely different biological scales whose intersection occurs in the nervous system.
There is another consequence that I think is particularly relevant to abstraction.
Suppose abstraction requires encountering multiple superdifferent instances of the same underlying relation. An isolated individual has only its own experiences from which to obtain those instances.
But a socially developing animal can receive other individuals' abstractions. Suddenly it doesn't need to independently encounter every example necessary to discover the relation.
And a genetic/developmental system can bias attention toward distinctions that were repeatedly fitness-relevant to ancestors without transmitting their explicit memories.
So there are at least three radically different ways past information can constrain present cognition:
I experienced it. → memory
Someone else experienced it. → social/cultural transmission
Ancestors carrying developmental biases toward it reproduced disproportionately. → genetic inheritance
The third does not transmit learned propositions; selection changes inherited architectures and predispositions. That's an important boundary because otherwise we'd accidentally wander into Lamarckian inheritance. But functionally, all three allow the present organism to behave differently because of information generated by past organism–environment interactions.
And this makes me reconsider your earlier statement that "it's all in the trade-offs."
Maybe one of the major evolutionary trade-offs isn't simply memory versus computation.
It could be where persistence is purchased.
A lineage has several places in which useful structure can persist:
synapses, other individuals, culture, or inherited developmental architecture.
Maintaining each has different costs and advantages. Long-lived socially dependent vertebrates can invest enormously in the middle two. Short-lived, minimally parental cephalopods can't exploit intergenerational social transmission nearly as effectively, so selection may disproportionately optimize what can be rebuilt reliably from inherited architecture plus rapid individual experience.
And now your mysterious third loop containing abstraction need not even fit entirely inside the animal.
That is the part I would flag most strongly before we try filling in its other terms. We may have been searching for three neural capacities when the missing terms could instead include something like communication, social modelling, inheritance, developmental plasticity, or transmission fidelity.
The location of the loop's closure is itself now an empirical variable.
That's a much bigger change to the model than merely adding genetics. It means the individual organism may be a node in the cognitive evolutionary architecture rather than the boundary of it.
Or more accurately, A specific configuration of and sequenced arrangentment between any number of the necessary nodes, so long as the remaining nodes and necessary connections between them exist externally and occur in the proper sequence to sustain continuity within the specific environment. One possible data point that would support this, vastly different rates of epigenetic alterations between generations in cephalopods compared to humans and really all other complex animals in general, or something similar. Or it would at least be evidence of one of several prerequisites for this theory to be possible
Yes. That's the more exact systems formulation. The organism isn't necessarily the loop. It can be one temporarily instantiated configuration of nodes in a loop whose remaining nodes reside elsewhere.
What has to persist is not a particular component but the ordered causal continuity:
Given nodes (N_1...N_n), a viable configuration only needs each required transition to occur somewhere, at the appropriate point in the sequence, with enough fidelity that the sequence closes and can recur.
That permits some very different implementations. One species might close most of the sequence neurally within an individual. Another might close part of it through parent–offspring interaction. Another through social groups. Another across generations through inherited developmental architecture. Different organisms could therefore instantiate the same functional capacity sequence while locating its nodes in completely different substrates and timescales.
And your proposed cephalopod datum is exactly the kind of observation we'd want, although I searched because there's a fascinating complication: I don't think the necessary comparative epigenetic dataset presently exists well enough to make the human–cephalopod claim. Mammalian transgenerational epigenetic inheritance itself remains contentious because mammals undergo extensive germline/embryonic epigenetic reprogramming; environmentally induced marks usually don't simply pass intact through generations.
But cephalopods have something arguably more interesting for your hypothesis than the specific epigenetic result you proposed.
Cephalopods are extraordinary RNA editors
Coleoid cephalopods perform exceptionally extensive A-to-I RNA editing, particularly in nervous-system transcripts. And this isn't merely static diversification. In Octopus bimaculoides, changing environmental temperature caused editing changes at tens of thousands of RNA sites; thousands altered protein-coding sequences, including proteins important to neural function. Some changes began within hours and stabilized over days.
That's not transgenerational inheritance. The edited RNA isn't equivalent to rewriting the germline genome, and we shouldn't use it as evidence that acquired information passes to offspring.
But structurally it is extremely provocative for what you're proposing.
The ordinary simplified picture is:
genotype → nervous-system phenotype → environmental interaction
Cephalopods have unusually powerful machinery allowing something more like:
genome → editable neural transcriptome ↔ environment
The genome specifies machinery whose realized molecular implementation can be altered rapidly in response to current environmental conditions. Temperature alone has experimentally demonstrated exactly this sort of environmentally responsive neural-protein recoding.
So your theory shouldn't actually predict merely "cephalopods should have more epigenetic inheritance." That's unnecessarily specific.
It predicts something broader and better:
Species that cannot close an important capacity loop through prolonged individual development, parental transmission, or social/cultural continuity should disproportionately possess some alternative mechanism capable of supplying the missing continuity or adaptive flexibility.
Then several mechanisms become candidate implementations rather than assumptions:
genetic selection, epigenetic inheritance, developmental programs, RNA editing, innate sensorimotor architecture, perhaps others.
That gives us a way of potentially falsifying the idea rather than continually accommodating observations.
If short-lived, non-parentally taught, cognitively exceptional animals systematically lack compensatory mechanisms—nothing unusual about inherited architecture, developmental plasticity, molecular adaptability, rapid individual learning, etc.—while animals with equivalent ecological constraints routinely function just fine without them, then this proposed closure requirement gets much weaker.
Conversely, if removing one route to continuity repeatedly predicts disproportionate investment in another, that's quite interesting.
And there's a deeper implication of your correction that I think is worth making explicit.
We've been calling A, B and hypothetical C "capacity loops." But perhaps their defining property isn't their components.
It's their closure condition.
Two species needn't possess identical nodes. They need to preserve the same necessary relations between functions. If one internal node disappears, an external node can substitute provided that the causal sequence still closes.
That would mean the proper comparative unit isn't:
brain architecture,
organism,
or even species trait.
It's something closer to a closed functional pathway distributed across organism × environment × time.
And suddenly the weird contrasts we've accumulated—human helplessness, bird parental care, cetacean social development, octopus short lifespans, radically distributed cephalopod nervous systems, memory versus abstraction, genetic versus cultural continuity—stop being annoying exceptions.
They're exactly what we'd expect to examine if evolution can solve the same closure requirement by relocating different portions of the sequence outside the individual.
The RNA-editing result makes me considerably more interested in actually testing this rather than merely developing it conceptually, because we already have at least one spectacular cephalopod-specific example of unusually extensive environmentally responsive molecular flexibility precisely in the nervous system. That's not confirmation—but it is the kind of independent observation you'd want to encounter at this stage.
I'll take your excitedness as a sign we've got all the concepts necessary to the idea present in the conversation. Because I felt exactly the same instinct when you began typing that reply but before you mentioned testing. Do as deep of a dive as it will let you do into research about any of the individual potential connections we made, especially the earlier ones and the heavily assumed ones, because the later ones are less likely to have research available by default.
Worked for 49s

Yes. I went after this as a collection of separate hypotheses rather than trying to find "our theory" in the literature. That was the right approach, because quite a few individual pieces have surprisingly strong support, while several of the links between them remain almost completely untested.

The overall result is encouraging in a very particular way: I did not find an existing theory that already assembles these observations into the closed, cross-scale architecture we've been developing. But most of the early biological premises needed to make that architecture plausible have empirical support. More importantly, some observations I found independently predict relationships we derived before knowing the literature.

1. The bird result is probably the strongest unexpected support

One of our earliest suspicions was:

prolonged parental provisioning / extended post-hatching development ↔ increased neural investment ↔ increased cognitive capability

There is a remarkably direct comparative test.

A 2023 PNAS study examined 1,176 bird species and found that measures of parental provisioning—including altricial versus precocial development, egg investment, and duration of provisioning—strongly predict relative brain size. The authors explicitly argue that parental provisioning permits offspring to overcome the energetic constraints of growing large brains.

Even more strikingly, an independent 2022 analysis covering neuron counts across 111 bird species found that pallial neuron number predicts innovation propensity, and that increased pallial neuron numbers covary with longer post-hatching developmental periods.

That is awfully close to one of our earliest chains:

extended protected development → greater opportunity/energy for neural development → more neural computational capacity → increased behavioral innovation

But notice what the research does not establish. It doesn't tell us whether parental care causes intelligence, whether intelligence selects for prolonged parental care, or whether they recursively reinforce one another.

Which is precisely why your refusal to impose an arrow on the sequence was justified.

Birds also solve our "tiny brain" problem

Corvids and parrots don't achieve sophisticated cognition by simply having mammalian-style large brains in miniature. Large-brained parrots and corvids pack extraordinarily large numbers of neurons into their forebrains—numbers comparable to or exceeding those of some primates with substantially larger brains.

So:

brain mass ≠ neural computational investment.

Distribution, density and allocation matter.

That supports your original emphasis on imbalance/specialization of nervous-system investment, rather than simple brain size.

───
2. The memory-specialization idea is extremely well supported in birds

You predicted that memory capacity might belong to its own capacity loop rather than simply being a component of intelligence.

Bird research provides almost a textbook natural experiment.

Food-storing passerines that must relocate huge numbers of caches tend to have disproportionately enlarged hippocampal systems relative to related non-storing species. Hippocampal damage impairs cache-location memory, and comparative studies have found more accurate and/or persistent spatial memory in some scatter-hoarding species.

And this specialization develops differently even between related corvid species with different caching requirements.

There has been legitimate controversy about exactly how universal and clean the hippocampus–caching relationship is, so it shouldn't be treated as an invariant law.

But the broad phenomenon stands:

A lineage can invest disproportionately in a specialized memory system in response to an ecological demand for retention.

That is important because it immediately gives us animals possessing exceptional memory specialization without necessarily possessing correspondingly exceptional domain-general abstraction.

So our distinction:

memory capacity ≠ general cognitive capacity ≠ abstraction

has real comparative-neuroscience support.

And birds provide something else we predicted: specialization of plasticity itself. Adult birds add neurons to specific forebrain systems; in songbirds, new neurons are incorporated into specialized song-control circuitry, and some seasonal species partially reconstruct portions of those circuits.

This is very different from "the entire brain becomes more plastic."

Evolution can allocate plasticity selectively.

───
3. Cephalopod memory is stronger—and stranger—than our extreme hypothetical

Our zero-memory cephalopod thought experiment does not survive existing evidence.

The octopus vertical lobe is demonstrably involved in learning and long-term memory, and it exhibits activity-dependent long-term potentiation remarkably similar in functional principle to vertebrate synaptic memory systems.

Even better for our model, experiments suggest a functional separation between short- and long-term memory mechanisms. Manipulation of vertical-lobe pathways changes learning rate and subsequent long-term recall differently.

That is almost exactly the sort of internal differentiation implied by your second loop:

working representation ↔ short-term retention ↔ durable long-term retention

Not proof of that sequence, obviously. But evidence that "memory" is not one undifferentiated capacity even within an octopus.

Cuttlefish go considerably further.They can behaviorally retain integrated information about:

what happened + where + when.

That capacity remains surprisingly intact into senescence.

And a 2024 study found evidence suggestive of false visual memories in cuttlefish. The authors interpret this as possible evidence that cephalopod memory can involve reconstructive processes rather than merely fixed stimulus-response retrieval.

That last finding particularly matters for your caution that animal "memory" needn't resemble human memory.

You're still right.

"episodic-like" is deliberately used because behavioral what–where–when performance does not demonstrate the phenomenological human experience of remembering oneself in a past event.

But the false-memory result makes a purely simple sensory-trigger model harder to maintain for all cephalopod memory.

───
4. Your arm-memory hypothesis remains remarkably open

Here's something I found genuinely striking: we apparently know much more about how octopus arms process information than about whether they retain durable learned information locally.

Octopus arms contain specialized chemotactile receptors permitting extremely rich contact-dependent "taste-by-touch" processing. Distinct sensory cell populations encode chemical and mechanical information locally.

The peripheral system can perform substantial autonomous sensorimotor processing.

But experiments also demonstrate that the central nervous system receives and uses arm-specific tactile and movement information during learned tasks. An arm is therefore neither merely a puppet nor clearly an independent cognitive unit.

What I did not find was convincing direct evidence of:

long-lived memory traces stored specifically within individual arm nervous systems whose loss would selectively eliminate learned information.

That looks like a genuine gap.

There is active connectomic work on sucker and arm ganglia, but these peripheral circuits remain comparatively poorly understood.

So your distinction remains completely legitimate:

local processing ability
does not entail
local memory storage.

And experimentally distinguishing them could reveal a great deal about what distributed nervous systems are actually distributing.

───
5. The abstraction variable turns out to be even more important—and harder to define

Research on comparative cognition strongly supports your instinct that abstraction shouldn't simply be treated as the upper end of "learning."

Animals can succeed through several increasingly general mechanisms:

specific association → categorization → relational learning → abstraction across novel exemplars.

Researchers explicitly warn that tests of abstract concepts must demonstrate transfer to new examples; otherwise sophisticated associative learning can masquerade as abstraction.

This is exactly the distinction you were making with the hypothetical octopus recognizing an enormous sensory profile.

And birds provide excellent positive controls.

Crows have demonstrated spontaneous relational matching—responding to the relationship between stimuli rather than their particular physical identities—in a paradigm generally interpreted as analogical reasoning.

Grey parrots have demonstrated cross-modal and symbolic relational abilities involving number and ordinal relations.

Now here's the significant asymmetry:

I found lots of evidence for cephalopod conditional discrimination, reversal learning, spatial learning, associative learning, episodic-like retention and flexible behavioral strategies.

I did not find comparably compelling literature establishing broad, cross-domain relational abstraction in cephalopods on the level of the strongest crow/parrot work.

That absence isn't evidence they lack it. Cephalopods are experimentally difficult, the literature is much smaller, sensory ecology makes vertebrate-oriented tests problematic, and individual variability is enormous.

But it's exactly the empirical gap our model predicts should matter.

───
6. Cephalopods also prevent us from equating intelligence with one single capacity

One study I especially like for this reason tested episodic-like memory in seven common octopuses.

Only one acquired the complete what–where–when strategy. The others relied on simpler but effective strategies such as familiarity avoidance, alternation, win-stay rules or risk sensitivity. There was considerable variation both between individuals and within individuals over time.

That's remarkable for our purposes.

Same broad nervous-system architecture.

Same species.

Same experimental problem.

Different functional strategies.

It argues against assuming that sophisticated behavior reveals a single underlying cognitive mechanism.

Similarly, cuttlefish can delay gratification for roughly 50–130 seconds under experimental conditions, and individuals capable of longer delays also performed better on reversal learning.

So certain capacities do covary.

But that doesn't mean they are identical.

This increasingly looks like a configuration problem, exactly as you just reformulated it: particular combinations of capacities can generate outwardly similar levels of behavioral sophistication.

───
7. One of our strongest assumptions about human altriciality needs modification

Humans really are unusual developmentally, but the conventional claim "humans are the most altricial mammals" is wrong.

A 2024 comparative analysis of 140 placental mammals concluded that humans exhibit an exceptionally rapid evolutionary shift toward lower neonatal brain maturity relative to adult brain size, but that this results primarily from enormous postnatal brain growth. Only a relatively small number of neurodevelopmental events appear actually to have been shifted from prenatal into postnatal development during hominin evolution.

So our useful variable is probably not simple altriciality.

The better variable is something like:

duration and magnitude of environmentally exposed neural maturation.

That fits our argument better anyway.

Humans exhibit prolonged postnatal brain development, extended synaptogenesis/myelination trajectories and extraordinary dependence on social learning. Human brain plasticity appears evolutionarily specialized for responsiveness to social/environmental experience.

So our original intuition survives, but with better precision.

───
8. Sociality itself is insufficient—but social information retention looks much stronger

Cetaceans provide another useful test.

Across cetacean species, encephalization correlates with measures of social structure and breadth of socially learned/cultural behavior. Larger-brained cetaceans tend to possess richer social and cultural repertoires.

That supports some connection among:

neural investment ↔ social complexity ↔ information transmission

but not the simplistic:

social animals become intelligent.

Plenty of highly social animals aren't dolphins.

Likewise, comparative researchers have explicitly moved away from a pure "social brain" explanation toward cultural intelligence models emphasizing social learning, behavioral flexibility and the expense of neural tissue.

That resonates much more strongly with our architecture because the relevant environmental node isn't mere group membership.

It is access to information generated by other minds.

───
9. The intergenerational-continuity idea produced the strangest result

Your suggestion was:

perhaps cephalopods should show unusually strong epigenetic or comparable mechanisms if some portions of their relevant capacity sequence close across generations rather than through parental/social transmission.

I specifically searched for this.

The direct epigenetic prediction is currently unsupported.

Cephalopods have conserved DNA methylation and histone-regulatory machinery, but measured DNA methylation levels in several cephalopods appear lower than vertebrate levels, not obviously dramatically greater. The field is young, and transgenerational cephalopod epigenetics is nowhere near developed enough to make a serious comparative claim.

So:

exceptional cephalopod transgenerational epigenetic inheritance: presently unknown/not established.

That is an important negative result.

But then comes the RNA editing.

───
10. Cephalopod RNA editing fits the broader prediction almost unnervingly well

Behaviorally sophisticated coleoid cephalopods perform extraordinary levels of A-to-I RNA editing, especially in neural tissues.

A major 2017 study found tens of thousands of evolutionarily conserved recoding sites concentrated in genes involved in neuronal excitability and morphology.

And there is a literal trade-off.

Maintaining editable RNA structures constrains the underlying DNA sequences around those sites. The authors found reduced genomic mutation/polymorphism around extensively edited regions and explicitly characterized the phenomenon as a:

trade-off between genome evolution and transcriptome plasticity.

That phrase could practically have fallen out of our conversation.

It means coleoid evolution has apparently paid some loss in genomic sequence flexibility to preserve an unusually powerful post-transcriptional mechanism for altering neural proteins.

That doesn't transmit learned memories.

It does something structurally closer to another possibility we identified:

preserve an inherited architecture capable of producing large amounts of adaptive variation during the individual's lifetime.

So our original specific hypothesis:

cephalopods compensate through unusually strong epigenetic inheritance

doesn't currently have support.

But the broader hypothesis:

their life-history architecture should be associated with unusual alternative mechanisms of adaptive flexibility

has a spectacular candidate.

───
11. Which brings us to the "trade-offs" idea

The research is increasingly consistent with thinking in terms of allocation rather than scalar intelligence.

There are finite biological resources:

energy, neurons, developmental time, lifespan, physical volume, parental investment, genomic flexibility, sensory bandwidth, connectivity, etc.

And different lineages appear to allocate those differently.

Birds can purchase:

extreme neuronal density + parental provisioning + long post-hatching neural development.

Humans purchase:

very prolonged development + tremendous durable memory + social transmission + abstraction + cultural accumulation.

Food-storing birds purchase:

disproportionately specialized spatial memory machinery.

Cephalopods purchase:

enormous peripheral sensorimotor processing + extraordinary sensory specialization + central learning machinery + unusually extensive neural RNA editing, while generally receiving little parental instruction and often living comparatively short lives.

Those aren't necessarily different amounts of one thing called intelligence.

They may be different solutions in a constrained multidimensional design space.

───
12. There is direct evidence for the independence of some loops

This might be the single most useful conclusion.

The literature already
 dissociations of the kind our architecture predicts.

Excellent memory does not guarantee exceptional abstraction.

Excellent abstraction does not require a mammalian cortex.

Large brains aren't required for very high forebrain neuron counts.

Strong sociality does not guarantee large brains.

Sophisticated learning does not require parental teaching.

Complex behavior does not guarantee the same underlying strategy even within one species.

Long-term memory and short-term learning can involve distinguishable neural mechanisms.

Central and peripheral processing can coexist without telling us where durable information is stored.

That makes a one-dimensional "intelligence" variable increasingly inadequate for the question we're asking.

───
13. Your two-loop architecture has survived remarkably well

I'd now write the two established candidate sequences roughly as you did:

Loop A — environment/action/cognition

--( sensory differentiation ↔ environmental manipulation ↔ cognitive capacity )--<

The evidence strongly supports interactions among these, but not a universal causal ordering.

Cephalopod chemotactile specialization and distributed sensorimotor processing are particularly good examples.

Loop B — retention

--( detailed working representation ↔ short-term retention/fidelity ↔ durable long-term retention )--<

The exact partitioning needs serious neuroscientific refinement, but distinct short/long-term memory machinery and specialized memory allocation are well established enough to justify treating memory architecture independently from generic cognition.

And the evidence supports your × intuition more than an additive model in one limited sense:

improvements in one domain frequently alter the evolutionary value of investment in another. Parental provisioning changes the energetic feasibility of neural development; neuron count predicts innovation; caching demands predict memory-system specialization; cephalopod RNA plasticity trades directly against genomic evolvability.

Whether the resulting dynamics are literally multiplicative remains a mathematical hypothesis.

───
14. Loop C is now the genuinely interesting unknown

I agree with our decision not to fill it in prematurely.

We have one particularly interesting candidate node:

abstraction / relational representation.

But here's what the literature suggests we need to distinguish.

Abstraction could mean:

category abstraction — ignoring irrelevant differences.

relational abstraction — recognizing A:B as structurally equivalent to C:D.

cross-modal abstraction — recognizing the same relation through different sensory channels.

counterfactual representation — representing unavailable alternatives.

temporal abstraction — reasoning across past/future states.

Those may themselves dissociate.

Crows provide surprisingly strong evidence for relational abstraction.

Parrots provide symbolic/ordinal abstraction.

Scrub-jays provide sophisticated memory and evidence of future-oriented behavior, although even within corvids closely related species don't uniformly reproduce the same planning results.

Cephalopods provide abundant evidence of learning, behavioral flexibility, delayed gratification, conditional discrimination and memory—but much less decisive evidence concerning high-order cross-context relational abstraction.

That makes abstraction one of the best variables to investigate next.

───
15. And your newest correction may be the most consequential

The biggest conceptual change from the research isn't neurological.

It's our realization that the boundaries of each loop don't have to coincide with the boundaries of an organism.

Nothing I've found contradicts that. In fact, several well-established phenomena make it almost unavoidable.

Parental provisioning can enable neural architecture that couldn't otherwise energetically develop.

Social learning gives an animal information generated by another nervous system.

Culture permits information to persist beyond the originating individual.

Genetic evolution preserves developmental architectures across generations.

RNA editing can permit inherited molecular machinery to generate environmentally responsive neural variation within an individual.

Consequently, your latest formulation is the best one:

An individual animal need only instantiate a particular configuration and ordering of the necessary nodes, provided that the missing nodes and required connections occur externally, in the correct sequence and timescale, sufficiently reliably to maintain closure.

That is not established evolutionary theory in the specific cognitive form we're giving it.

But its component mechanisms are absolutely real.

───
Where I would grade the hypothesis right now

Strongly supported prerequisites: specialized neural investment exists; memory systems can evolve independently/specially; sensory systems and behavioral control co-specialize; parental provisioning predicts neural development in birds; neural allocation predicts innovation; cephalopods genuinely learn and remember; cephalopods have radically distributed sensorimotor processing; cognition can evolve without parental teaching; neural plasticity can itself be anatomically specialized.

Supported but causally unresolved: prolonged development ↔ neural investment ↔ cognition; social transmission ↔ brain/cognitive complexity; sensory differentiation ↔ manipulation ↔ cognitive sophistication; memory sophistication ↔ general cognitive flexibility.

Plausible and genuinely open: abstraction occupying a distinct capacity sequence; individual lineages distributing required nodes differently between organism/environment; compensatory trade-offs among memory, sensory precision, online computation, abstraction and external information transmission; loop closure occurring across different temporal scales.

Currently unsupported: cephalopods having unusually strong transgenerational epigenetic inheritance; durable memories residing specifically in individual octopus arms; a universal multiplicative law connecting the candidate loops.

Actively contradicted in its strongest form: cephalopods possessing almost no memory. The evidence for durable, integrated and perhaps reconstructive memory is too strong for that.

And then there's the most interesting finding of the whole search:

The literature seems to contain the pieces, but not the architecture.

There are entire research traditions studying life-history evolution, cognitive ecology, parental provisioning, comparative memory, neural specialization, social brain/cultural intelligence, cephalopod cognition, distributed nervous systems, RNA editing, and relational abstraction.

They mostly ask questions within those categories.

What we've accidentally started asking is:

What minimal set of capacity sequences must close, by any combination of internal, environmental, social and hereditary nodes, for selection to sustain an accelerating regime of increasingly sophisticated cognition?

That's a different question.

And I now think the cleanest next move isn't more theorizing. It is to construct a comparative matrix of perhaps 30–50 strategically chosen species—not ranked by intelligence—with columns for parental provisioning, juvenile dependence, lifespan, social transmission, nervous-system centralization, sensory differentiation, manipulatory degrees of freedom, memory specialization, long-term retention, relational abstraction, neuron allocation/density, adult neurogenesis, molecular plasticity, and so forth.

Then we look for dissociations.

The animals that break apparent correlations will tell us what belongs in different loops.

The animals in which capacities repeatedly travel together will tell us what might belong in the same loop.

And the cases where an internal capacity disappears while some external mechanism appears to replace its function are exactly where we'd begin testing your distributed closure proposition.

After this dive, I'd say we've reached the point where that comparison would produce more information than another round of pure conceptual elaboration.
